# Unix line endings everywhere, in the working copy as well as in the repository.
#
# **Not a matter of taste.** Line endings leak out of files and into things that are compared:
#
#   - Five migrations insert text — the Blind-Date rules among it. Checked out with CRLF, the same
#     migration seeded that text *with* carriage returns, so a Windows checkout produced different
#     rows than the Linux server. Invisible from outside: same wording, same line count, four bytes
#     longer, a different checksum. It cost an hour to establish that nobody had edited anything.
#   - `deno fmt --check` refuses a CRLF file with „Text differed by line endings", so
#     `validate:check` failed locally while passing in CI. A check that is red for a reason nobody
#     acts on is a check that stops being read.
#   - Seeds, fixtures and documents have the same property the moment their content reaches a
#     database, a snapshot or an assertion.
#
# The repository itself does not change: every tracked file is already stored with LF — measured,
# not assumed. What changes is what lands in the working copy on the next checkout, on every
# platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the exception, and the only one: the Windows command interpreter reads them
# line by line as it executes, and a `goto` in a file with Unix endings can land in the wrong
# place. These two start the development servers.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Spelled out although the rule above already covers it, because this is the one where getting it
# wrong writes wrong data rather than producing an awkward diff.
database/migrations/*.sql text eol=lf
